### P1 — Decide which 16 tours should become full supported runtimes

- **Who:** OwnASquare product owner with each app's runtime owner.
- **What:** Replace selected read-only tours with deterministic interactive
  demos or supported full hosted runtimes.
- **How discovered:** Runtime classification identified 16 products that depend
  on Python, local models, private providers, autonomous execution, or other
  server-side capabilities unsuitable for a truthful static preview.
- **Where:** The corresponding app repositories and preview Workers listed by
  `scripts/deploy-remaining-previews.mjs`.
- **When:** Per product priority and only after the hosted runtime has an
  explicit security, data, cost, and reliability design.
- **Detailed description:** Keep the visible tour label until real execution
  is deployed and proven. A deterministic synthetic demo is acceptable when it
  explains its simulation boundary; a fake provider or model response is not.
- **Acceptance criteria:**
  - Product-specific hosting choice is documented.
  - Auth, data retention, secrets, cost limits, and failure behavior are defined.
  - The preview label matches the actual runtime.
  - GitHub source remains reachable.
- **Definition of done:** Each selected app has either a proven full runtime or
  an explicitly accepted tour/demo boundary.
- **Reason:** Public preview availability and full product execution are
  different product decisions.
- **Priority:** P1.
- **Why:** These apps provide more value when their core flow can run safely.
- **Regression prevention:** UI assertions require the tour/demo/full-runtime
  label to agree with deployment metadata.
- **Testing:** Playwright workflow, provider failure, data isolation, security,
  accessibility, responsive, and no-console-error suites.
- **Audit policies:** Truthful capability labeling, least privilege, data
  minimization, and evidence-qualified release claims.
- **Other considerations:** Do not add paid-provider calls without budget and
  abuse controls.

### P2 — Add durable fleet health and isolated-config auditing

- **Who:** OwnASquare platform maintainer.
- **What:** Continuously verify every preview's canonical host, Worker routing,
  source URL, and isolated deployment configuration.
- **How discovered:** The first remaining-fleet run inherited the parent
  Wrangler file and temporarily attached apex routes to four preview Workers.
- **Where:** Parent scripts/tests, CI or scheduled monitor, and Cloudflare route
  readback.
- **When:** Before and after every fleet deployment, then on a bounded schedule.
- **Detailed description:** Treat the generated per-app configuration as a
  required release input and reject any preview config that contains apex,
  `www`, an extra hostname, or an unexpected account selector.
- **Acceptance criteria:**
  - Every preview config contains exactly one canonical custom domain.
  - Apex and `www` remain owned only by `ownasquare-platform`.
  - Fifty preview URLs and fifty source URLs pass.
  - A failed item stops the batch with a clear slug and command boundary.
- **Definition of done:** Automated preflight and postflight catch route drift
  before catalog promotion.
- **Reason:** Wrangler searches parent directories for configuration unless an
  explicit isolated file is passed.
- **Priority:** P2.
- **Why:** Route bleed can replace the main site even when app assets are valid.
- **Regression prevention:** Keep `--config` mandatory and test generated
  config contents.
- **Testing:** Node contract tests, Wrangler dry run, provider route inventory,
  and HTTP verification.
- **Audit policies:** Narrow mutation scope, exact hostname ownership,
  stop-on-first-failure, and production readback.
- **Other considerations:** Keep `.preview-build/` ignored and reproducible.

